# conversations-tagalog-chat.kb - Conversations / Tagalog chat
# Format: concept | intent | definition | keywords | patterns | tl | bis
#
# WHY THIS FILE EXISTS
#
# The other conversation shelves explain IDEAS - what banter is, what an
# inside joke is, what "hang out" means. They are all written in English
# about English, and a person whose day is conducted in Tagalog gets
# fluent answers about a language they are not thinking in, to questions
# they did not ask.
#
# What was missing is the actual chat. The sentences people really send:
# "busy ako", "puntahan kita", "kinakabahan ako na", "kumain ka na",
# "ang init ngayon". These are not vocabulary questions and they are not
ang init | definition | Ang init is a complaint delivered as a statement of fact, and it is the default Filipino response to weather that is too much. Nothing is being asked, so answering with advice about hydration is answering a different conversation. The pattern is worth recognising across languages: a remark with no question mark often wants agreement and company rather than a solution, and treating it as a request is how small talk dies. | hot, weather, heat, complain, remark, fact, agreement, company, small talk | what does ang init mean, how to reply to ang init, is ang init a question, bakit ang init | ang init | maayo na kaayo
nanghinihiya ako | definition | Nanghinihiya ako is embarrassed, and like kinakabahan it is the progressive form, marking something happening to the speaker right now rather than a settled trait. The distinction from hiya is worth keeping: hiya is closer to shame, a more durable thing, while nanghinihiya is the ordinary social embarrassment of saying or doing something awkward. In practice the person is usually telling you what happened and expects you not to make it larger. | embarrassed, shame, awkward, progressive, social, now, durable, reaction | what does nanghinihiya ako mean, difference between hiya and kahihiya, how to reply when embarrassed | nanghinihiya ako | maa ang akong kab-iya
tama ka | definition | Tama ka is you are right, and in ordinary conversation it very often means the opposite - I am agreeing so we can stop arguing. Filipino speakers distinguish the honest agreement from the conversational one by tone and by whether they add anything after it, and the difference matters when someone is asking whether you actually agree with them. A bare tama ka followed by silence is closer to the second meaning than the first. | right, correct, agree, disagree, stop, tone, silence, concession, meaning | what does tama ka mean, is tama ka always agreement, how to disagree politely in tagalog | tama ka | husto ka
nag-aalala ako sa iyo | definition | Nag-aalala ako sa iyo is a declaration of concern that is not a request, and it is one of the warmer things said in Filipino without being romantic. The progressive nag- marks it as current, and the sa iyo is what turns a general worry into a personal one. Someone saying it is usually checking that the feeling was received, so an acknowledgement lands better than information. | worry, concern, care, declare, feel, receive, acknowledge, information, warm | what does nag-aalala ako sa iyo mean, how to reply to nag-aalala ako sa iyo, is it romantic | nag-aalala ako sa iyo | nabahala ako sa imong kaugnayan
okay lang ako | definition | Okay lang ako is the Filipino all-clear, and it is used in two opposite directions that have to be read from the situation. Spoken as a reply it means I am fine, genuinely. Spoken as a preamble it means I am coping, do not worry - and the person is usually not fine, which is why the two are not interchangeable. Someone asking okay ka na after clearly having a hard time is offering the second meaning whether or not that was intended. | okay, fine, all-clear, coping, not fine, reassure, reply, preamble, context | what does okay lang ako mean, is okay lang ako always true, how to reply to okay lang ako | okay lang ako | okay gyud ako
susulongin kita | definition | Susulongin kita is a promise about the future, and it carries more weight than the English promise to keep in touch because it names a specific act rather than a general intention. The sus- prefix is the future, and the object is the person rather than the thing, so the promise is about showing up. It is the sort of sentence people say when they are about to be busy or difficult, which is exactly when it is most likely to be forgotten. | promise, future, keep in touch, show up, busy, difficult, forget, specific, weight | what does susulongin kita mean, is susulongin kita a promise, how to reply to susulongin kita | susulongin kita | makatabang ako nimo

# small talk in the abstract - they are status updates, permissions,
# reassurances, and complaints, and they are what a conversation is
# actually made of.
#
# Each entry below answers a real shape of message rather than defining a
# word, because that is the gap: a bank can define "busy" perfectly and
# still have nothing to say to somebody who says "busy ako".
#
# 12 entries.

busy ako | definition | Busy in Tagalog is usually not a request for anything. It is a status report, and the useful reply is neither advice nor a question about the work. The word comes from English and has kept the same meaning, so "busy ako" is the ordinary way to say a person is occupied. Replying with "bakit busy ka" is the common mistake, because it asks for a justification the speaker was not offering. | busy, occupied, working, status, update, reply, workload, english loan | what does busy ako mean, how to reply to busy ako, is busy ako a question, paano sumagot sa busy ako | busy ako | busy ako
puntahan kita | definition | Puntahan kita means I will come over to you, and in practice it is a statement of intent rather than a request for permission, even though the grammar has no subject marker to say so. The same shape covers leaving - "alis na ako" - and both are announcements a person makes while already moving. Replying with a question about the timing tends to read as not having heard the message. | come, visit, going, over, leaving, announcement, plan, timing, intent | what does puntahan kita mean, is puntahan kita a request, how to reply to puntahan kita | puntahan kita | puntahan ako nimo
kinakabahan ako | definition | Kinakabahan ako is the Tagalog progressive of kabahan, and the progressive is doing real work here: it describes a state that is happening right now, which is different from kabahan ako as a general fact about yourself. The reason it needs care is that it is nearly always an opening move. Somebody who says it is usually looking for either reassurance or a listener, and the reliable answer acknowledges the feeling before asking a single practical question. | nervous, worried, anxious, afraid, progressive, now, reassurance, listen, opening | what does kinakabahan ako mean, how to reply to kinakabahan ako, is kinakabahan ako asking for help | kinakabahan ako | nakahadlok ako
kumain ka na ba | definition | Kumain ka na ba is the single most ordinary sentence in Filipino daily conversation, and it is a question that does not want an answer about food. It means have you eaten, and the real question underneath is a form of care. The variants matter for tone: "kumain ka na" is neutral, "kumain ka na ba" is a gentle check, and the question particle adds noticeable concern when aimed at an elder or someone unwell. | eaten, eat, food, meal, care, check, neutral, concern, tone | what does kumain ka na ba mean, how to reply to kumain ka na, is kumain ka na ba a real question | kumain ka na ba | naa ka na ba mag-igon
sige | definition | Sige is the Filipino yes that means go ahead, and it is short enough to pass as agreement when it is not. It accepts a plan, it acknowledges information without requiring a reply, and among people who know each other it can mean I heard you, I am not going to argue, which is not consent. The context decides which, and the difference matters when someone is relying on the answer. | yes, go ahead, agree, okay, accept, hear, consent, argument, context | what does sige mean, is sige consent, how to reply to sige, is sige a yes | sige | okay
pasensya na | definition | Pasensya na is sorry with a softener attached. The na marks it as settling something rather than opening a fresh complaint, so it is what you say when you have already explained once and want to move on. It is not the same as sorry as a standalone, which leaves the matter open. Filipino separates these two uses in a way English does not, and treating them as interchangeable can make an apology sound more final than the speaker meant. | sorry, apology, softener, settle, closed, open, move on, final, meaning | what does pasensya na mean, difference between pasensya and sorry, how to apologise in tagalog | pasensya na | duha sorry
